WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:07.870
The world is full of different foods, every
culture, region and era has their own distinct

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:08.870
cuisine.

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:14.980
But what makes some food taste good and other
food taste bad and why do different people

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:23.400
like different foods?

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.509
Let's start from the beginning.

00:00:25.509 --> 00:00:30.439
When you chew food it is broken down into
small pieces, these pieces are then broken

00:00:30.439 --> 00:00:36.670
down into even smaller pieces by enzymes contained
in your saliva.

00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:42.420
These small particles of food then move over
your papillae, which are small bumps coating

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:49.510
your entire tongue, each papillae contains
between 50 and 100 taste cells folded together

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:53.070
like the petals of a young flower about to
bloom.

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:55.640
Hence the name taste buds.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:01:00.800
The average human has about 10,000 taste buds
on their tongue.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:06.660
Taste buds contain chemical receptors which
have evolved over millions of years to react

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.320
to five basic tastes.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:12.370
These are, bitter, sweet, sour, salty and
unami.

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:18.980
Unami is a word borrowed from Japanese that
describes savoury flavours, such as roast

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:20.371
meat and soya sauce.

00:01:20.371 --> 00:01:27.850
So that's the basics out the way, but what
makes people prefer sweet tasting food like

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:31.780
ice cream over, say, raw cabbage?

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:36.770
It all has to do with evolution and the history
of the human race.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:42.330
Let's go back about 3 million years, at this
point in time humans hadn't yet developed

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:49.010
the basic tools required to hunt animals,
so they fed on foraged berries, nuts, herbs

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:50.660
and insects.

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:56.400
We've all heard the term "survival of the
fittest" but back then being fit meant having

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:02.470
a lot of energy, so naturally humans learnt
to seek out the foods with the highest energy

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:03.680
content.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:10.190
Out of all the food groups, fat and carbohydrates
give the most calories per gram.

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:15.680
This was even more important about a million
years later, when humans developed tools and

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:21.400
learnt how to hunt, because hunting animals
requires a large amount of energy.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:26.819
Sweet foods are laden with carbohydrates,
and 3 million years ago, the sweetest foods

00:02:26.819 --> 00:02:29.629
available were fruits and honey.

00:02:29.629 --> 00:02:36.319
But since fatty and sweet foods proved to
be very good for survival, our taste receptors

00:02:36.319 --> 00:02:42.439
evolved to provide a more pleasurable experience
when we eat such foods.

00:02:42.439 --> 00:02:47.850
Because it's in our bodies best interest to
signal to our brains, when we eat them, which

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:52.310
foods are beneficial and which could do us
harm.

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:59.189
Similarly our taste receptors evolved to react
negatively to sour and bitter foods because

00:02:59.189 --> 00:03:05.590
in the paleolithic era, certain berries and
other foods which had bitter and sour tastes

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:10.439
were poisonous to humans or contained unwanted
toxins.

00:03:10.439 --> 00:03:16.269
So our bodies developed "bad taste" as a defence
mechanism against such foods.

00:03:16.269 --> 00:03:24.489
It just so happens that the types of taste
our taste receptors evolved to react positively

00:03:24.489 --> 00:03:29.469
to such as sweet and salty, because they used
to be essential to our survival.

00:03:29.469 --> 00:03:36.359
And now these tastes are found in many foods
which are detrimental to our health in large

00:03:36.359 --> 00:03:38.139
quantities.

00:03:38.139 --> 00:03:43.590
In our modern age where we are sedentary for
most of the day, we no longer require such

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:47.700
a large amount of calories to go out hunting.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:54.761
So all those extra carbohydrates we don't
burn off, which, thanks to evolution, our

00:03:54.761 --> 00:04:00.249
taste buds just love, instead clog up our
arteries.

00:04:00.249 --> 00:04:07.360
On the other hand, our taste receptors react
negatively to healthy foods like vegetables,

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:14.650
because they taste bitter, which evolution
has told us, it must mean they're poisonous.

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:20.169
Because of this taste aversion, many people
choose to avoid such foods, even though they

00:04:20.169 --> 00:04:22.490
know they're good for us.

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:30.200
Until 50 years ago sweet and fatty foods were
relatively rare and costly so gorging on them

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:36.550
whenever we got the chance was essential to
survival, it's only in recent years where

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:41.860
fast food has become so readily available,
that what we have to eat to survive is no

00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:43.370
longer a concern.

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:51.990
As a result the number of obese people in
the U.S. tripled between 1960 and 2007.

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:56.419
Because our food choices are now determined
by what we fancy eating instead of what we

00:04:56.419 --> 00:05:00.060
can forage, hunt or afford.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:05.360
Some evolutionary biologists predict that
in the future our taste buds will evolve to

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:12.080
prefer the taste of foods that are beneficial
to our health rather than those laden with

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:13.560
fat and sugar.

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:20.650
Which means in 100,000 years McDonald's most
popular burger might be the McCabbage.

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:26.460
Okay, that's an awful lot of rambling on about
what you should and shouldn't be eating.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:30.819
I'm sorry, here's a glazed doughnut to make
up for it.

00:05:30.819 --> 00:05:36.750
But we all have different taste preferences,
so why do some people think this doughnut

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:42.120
tastes amazing, whilst others would much rather
eat this.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:46.199
The surface of everyone's tongue is vastly
different.

00:05:46.199 --> 00:05:51.789
People who have a high density of taste buds
on their tongues are called supertasters,

00:05:51.789 --> 00:05:57.270
these people experience stronger flavours
than normal and often find flavours overwhelming.

00:05:57.270 --> 00:06:03.780
As a result, supertasters often order mild
food instead of spicy and drink their coffee

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:10.669
with milk and sugar instead of black, to tone
down the flavours for their highly sensitive

00:06:10.669 --> 00:06:11.669
palate.

00:06:11.669 --> 00:06:17.960
On the other hand, people with a low taste
bud density on their tongues are called subtasters,

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:25.150
subtasters find flavours weaker and will often
order very spicy food and heavily season their

00:06:25.150 --> 00:06:26.150
meals.

00:06:26.150 --> 00:06:32.759
But taste bud density isn't the whole story
as to why we all prefer different foods.

00:06:32.759 --> 00:06:39.810
Everyone's brain can recognise the five basic
tastes, bitter, sweet, salty, sour and unami.

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:45.610
However the group of chemicals that can trigger
those taste signals varies from person to

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:46.639
person.

00:06:46.639 --> 00:06:51.909
This arose from different evolutionary threats
in different parts of the world.

00:06:51.909 --> 00:06:57.580
Most toxic plants taste bitter, so in parts
of the world with a higher density of toxic

00:06:57.580 --> 00:07:04.099
plants human inhabitants would have adapted
to dislike the taste of bitter foods more,

00:07:04.099 --> 00:07:08.449
compared to people who lived in a less toxic
plant infested habitat.

00:07:08.449 --> 00:07:14.620
But there are lot's of environmental factors
that have attributed to such a variance in

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:19.120
taste receptors in different geographical
regions.

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:25.349
For example people from malaria infested regions
tend to carry a gene that makes them less

00:07:25.349 --> 00:07:28.620
sensitive to bitter compounds.

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:36.400
And millions of years of environmental factors
such as these as well as crossing gene pools

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:42.560
has led to a great variance in how peoples
taste receptors perceive flavours.

00:07:42.560 --> 00:08:12.959
Which is why some people find this too bitter,
whilst others love it.

